module Music exposing (..)

-- The whole soundtrack: drum cells, chords, the groove, and the lead line the
-- jumps play. THE WORLD plays everything -- cues are scheduled from the level,
-- not from the player, so the song sounds the same on a perfect run.

import Balance exposing (..)
import Types exposing (..)


-- No free-running tape. The game loop fires the groove ONE BEAT at a time
-- (a CueBeat when the player crosses each beat line: grooveBeat below
-- builds that beat's kick/snare/hat/bass/arp cells), and the HIGH LEAD as
-- one-shot cues at the exact jump positions. Both therefore track the
-- player's movement BY CONSTRUCTION: hide the tab and rAF stops the game,
-- so the music stops too and resumes exactly in phase (the old Sound.loop
-- tape kept playing on the audio clock while the game froze, and the lead
-- came back in the wrong part of the bar). It also means the arrangement
-- can GROW with the level: grooveBeat keys off the world beat, adding
-- layers section by section. Timing: ticks are vsync-locked (~16.7 ms at
-- 60 Hz), so 30 ticks = ~500 ms = one beat at 120 BPM; every eighth cell
-- below is exactly 250 ms so a beat is two cells.


-- --- the desk: where the parts stand ---
--
-- Kick, snare and bass stay CENTRE, and that is not laziness: low frequencies
-- carry almost no directional information, so a placed bass only makes the
-- whole piece lean. The two parts that actually mask each other are the bright
-- ones: the hats and the arp shimmer are both narrow and both live up top, so
-- they step out to opposite sides and each becomes audible on its own. The
-- melody opens around the middle as a detuned pair (cueSound below), which
-- leaves the centre of the tune still in the centre.
--
-- An Amiga would have thrown these hard to the walls (Paula panned its four
-- channels 2 and 2, with nothing in between). This is played on a phone at
-- arm's length or on headphones, so a third of the way out is where a part
-- separates without the piece tipping over.


hatPan : Int
hatPan = 0 - 26


arpPan : Int
arpPan = 30


leadPan : Int
leadPan = 20


-- --- the kit (recipes proven in the soundboard example: no Atari booms) ---
--
-- A struck thing loses energy in proportion to the energy it still has, and
-- Sound.decay is that fall. Without it a drum is a rectangle that comes up and
-- goes down, which is the loudest "this is synthetic" cue the engine has: a
-- kick held at full level for 80 ms is not a kick, it is an organ key on a low
-- note.
--
-- It is NOT on every hit here, and that is the point. A decay only says
-- something on a hit long enough to have a tail: the kick (85 ms), the snare
-- (80), the open hat (120). On the closed hats (30 and 26 ms), the ghost (40)
-- and the sixteenth roll (45) it is inaudible as SHAPE and audible only as
-- LEVEL, because a burst that short is already over before an envelope can say
-- anything about it. Those four kept the shape they had.
--
-- The decayed hits were re-gained afterwards, which is what a desk does: the
-- fall costs average energy, so the volume goes back up until the hit peaks
-- where it used to. Measured against the previous version, every transient is
-- within a decibel of where it was and the groove carries about 3 dB less
-- average energy. That difference IS the dynamics.
--
-- Nothing below changes LENGTH, because decay is shaped inside the hit it
-- wraps: every cell is still exactly 250 ms and the beat grid the level is
-- built on is untouched.


-- The sine is the kick's WEIGHT. A triangle sweeping 150 Hz to 45 has the right
-- shape, but a triangle spends part of its energy on harmonics, and on a laptop
-- speaker that reads as a click where a thump should be. The same sweep on a
-- sine underneath, at 45% of the triangle, puts the fundamental back. The decay
-- is the hit itself: full for an instant, then 18% for the rest of the note.
drKick : Sound
drKick =
    Sound.sequence
        [ Sound.decay 70 18
            (Sound.chord
                [ Sound.volume 55 (Sound.sweep 45 (Sound.tone Sound.Triangle 150 85))
                , Sound.volume 26 (Sound.sweep 45 (Sound.tone Sound.Sine 150 85))
                ]
            )
        , Sound.rest 165
        ]


-- Wires and shell, both falling: the noise cracks and the swept triangle rings
-- under it for a few more milliseconds. decay patches EVERY voice of the chord,
-- and it is clamped to each voice's own length, so one line shapes both.
drSnare : Sound
drSnare =
    Sound.sequence
        [ Sound.decay 55 18
            (Sound.chord
                [ Sound.volume 40 (Sound.tone Sound.Noise 0 80)
                , Sound.volume 17 (Sound.sweep 130 (Sound.tone Sound.Triangle 190 45))
                ]
            )
        , Sound.rest 170
        ]


-- The hats sit off to one side of centre, away from the arp. That placement is
-- the whole change here: 30 ms of noise is already a tick, and an envelope on
-- something that short only makes it quieter.
drHat : Sound
drHat =
    Sound.pan hatPan
        (Sound.sequence [ Sound.volume 11 (Sound.tone Sound.Noise 0 30), Sound.rest 220 ])


-- two sixteenths of hat in one eighth cell
drHat2 : Sound
drHat2 =
    Sound.pan hatPan
        (Sound.sequence
            [ Sound.volume 11 (Sound.tone Sound.Noise 0 30), Sound.rest 95
            , Sound.volume 9 (Sound.tone Sound.Noise 0 26), Sound.rest 99
            ]
        )


-- the open hat: the hand comes off, so this one is long enough to RING, and the
-- decay is what lets it ring down instead of stopping square
drOpen : Sound
drOpen =
    Sound.pan hatPan
        (Sound.sequence [ Sound.decay 95 20 (Sound.volume 17 (Sound.tone Sound.Noise 0 120)), Sound.rest 130 ])


-- a ghost note: a whisper of snare on the offbeat (one eighth cell)
drGhost : Sound
drGhost =
    Sound.sequence [ Sound.volume 15 (Sound.tone Sound.Noise 0 40), Sound.rest 210 ]


-- two sixteenth-note snare hits in one eighth cell (the fill roll)
dr16x2 : Sound
dr16x2 =
    Sound.sequence
        [ Sound.volume 26 (Sound.tone Sound.Noise 0 45), Sound.rest 80
        , Sound.volume 26 (Sound.tone Sound.Noise 0 45), Sound.rest 80
        ]


r8 : Sound
r8 =
    Sound.rest 250


-- --- bass: a syncopated Amiga-style line, triangle sub + a bite of saw ---
--
-- A tracker bass is PLUCKED, not held: the decay drops it to just over half in
-- 130 ms and lets it sit there, which is what separates two repeated eighths
-- into two notes instead of one long one. The triangle already carries the
-- fundamental, so this line needs no sine under it; the saw is only the bite on
-- top. It stays dead centre, where the weight of the groove belongs.


bs : Int -> Sound
bs hz =
    Sound.sequence
        [ Sound.decay 130 52
            (Sound.chord
                [ Sound.volume 30 (Sound.tone Sound.Triangle hz 210)
                , Sound.volume 7 (Sound.tone Sound.Sawtooth hz 210)
                ]
            )
        , Sound.rest 40
        ]


-- --- the tracker arpeggio: a chord cycled fast on one voice (Sound.arp),
-- one shimmering quarter note per beat ---
--
-- On a real tracker this is a plucked sample being retriggered, so it rings
-- down across the beat rather than sitting flat for 480 ms. It answers the hats
-- from the other side of the image.


ar : Int -> List Int -> Sound
ar hz shape =
    Sound.pan arpPan
        (Sound.sequence
            [ Sound.decay 340 45
                (Sound.volume 11 (Sound.arp shape (Sound.duty 25 (Sound.tone Sound.Square hz 480))))
            , Sound.rest 20
            ]
        )


-- --- harmony: one chord per 8 beats. Sections 1-4 ride Am F C G; the last
-- two swap the G for the E dominant to push toward the finish ---


-- `sh` is the arpeggio's shape in semitones above `ab`, which is what a chord
-- quality is: minor is [ 3, 7 ] and major is [ 4, 7 ], whatever the root.
type alias GChord =
    { r : Int, o : Int, f : Int, ab : Int, sh : List Int }


chAm : GChord
chAm = { r = Sound.a 2, o = Sound.a 3, f = Sound.e 3, ab = Sound.a 3, sh = [ 3, 7 ] }


chF : GChord
chF = { r = Sound.f 2, o = Sound.f 3, f = Sound.c 3, ab = Sound.f 3, sh = [ 4, 7 ] }


chC : GChord
chC = { r = Sound.c 3, o = Sound.c 4, f = Sound.g 3, ab = Sound.c 4, sh = [ 4, 7 ] }


chG : GChord
chG = { r = Sound.g 2, o = Sound.g 3, f = Sound.d 3, ab = Sound.g 3, sh = [ 4, 7 ] }


chE : GChord
chE = { r = Sound.e 2, o = Sound.e 3, f = Sound.b 2, ab = Sound.e 4, sh = [ 4, 7 ] }


chordAt : Int -> Int -> GChord
chordAt sec ph =
    if ph == 0 then chAm
    else if ph == 1 then chF
    else if ph == 2 then chC
    else if sec >= 4 then chE
    else chG


-- a beat = two 250ms eighth cells back to back
p2 : Sound -> Sound -> Sound
p2 a b =
    Sound.sequence [ a, b ]


-- ONE BEAT of the groove, keyed by the world beat you just crossed. The
-- arrangement grows with the level: section 1 is a bare pulse, the backbeat
-- lands in section 2, ghosts and drive in 3, the arp shimmer in 4, and the
-- last two sections run the full kit with double-kick, 16th hats and a
-- snare roll closing every section. Cells may ring past the beat line
-- (one-shots overlap freely), so seams stay clean at any granularity.
grooveBeat : Int -> Sound
grooveBeat b =
    let
        sec = min 8 (b // 32)
        lb = modBy 32 b
        k = modBy 4 lb
        c = chordAt sec (lb // 8)
        fill = (sec >= 1 && lb >= 30) || (sec >= 6 && lb >= 28)
        kick =
            if sec == 0 then
                if k == 0 || k == 2 then p2 drKick r8 else p2 r8 r8
            else if sec >= 6 then
                -- the storm sections: double kick all the way home
                if k == 0 || k == 2 then p2 drKick drKick else p2 r8 drKick
            else if sec >= 4 then
                if k == 0 || k == 2 then p2 drKick r8 else p2 r8 drKick
            else if k == 0 then
                p2 drKick r8
            else if k == 3 then
                p2 r8 r8
            else
                p2 r8 drKick
        snare =
            if sec == 0 then
                p2 r8 r8
            else if fill then
                if lb == 28 then p2 drSnare dr16x2
                else if lb == 29 then p2 dr16x2 dr16x2
                else if lb == 30 then p2 dr16x2 dr16x2
                else p2 drSnare drOpen
            else if k == 1 then
                p2 drSnare r8
            else if k == 3 then
                if sec >= 2 then p2 drSnare drGhost else p2 drSnare r8
            else
                p2 r8 r8
        hat =
            if sec == 0 then
                p2 drHat r8
            else if sec >= 6 then
                if k == 1 || k == 3 then p2 drOpen drHat2 else p2 drHat2 drHat2
            else if sec >= 4 then
                if k == 3 then p2 drOpen drHat2 else p2 drHat2 drHat2
            else if k == 3 then
                p2 drOpen drHat2
            else
                p2 drHat drHat
        bass =
            if sec == 0 then
                if k == 0 || k == 2 then p2 (bs c.r) r8 else p2 r8 r8
            else if sec >= 4 then
                if k == 0 then p2 (bs c.r) (bs c.r)
                else if k == 1 then p2 (bs c.r) (bs c.o)
                else if k == 2 then p2 (bs c.r) (bs c.r)
                else p2 (bs c.f) (bs c.o)
            else if k == 0 then
                p2 (bs c.r) r8
            else if k == 1 then
                p2 (bs c.r) (bs c.o)
            else if k == 2 then
                p2 r8 (bs c.r)
            else
                p2 r8 (bs c.o)
        arps =
            if sec >= 3 then [ ar c.ab c.sh ] else []
    in
    Sound.chord (List.concat [ [ kick, snare, hat, bass ], arps ])


-- melody contour: climbs the A-minor pentatonic and comes back down, one
-- step per jump: chains become runs, lone jumps get a single accent
leadPitch : Int -> Int
leadPitch i =
    let
        k = modBy 8 i
    in
    if k == 0 then Sound.a 4
    else if k == 1 then Sound.c 5
    else if k == 2 then Sound.d 5
    else if k == 3 then Sound.e 5
    else if k == 4 then Sound.g 5
    else if k == 5 then Sound.e 5
    else if k == 6 then Sound.d 5
    else Sound.c 5


-- jump beats per 32-beat segment, LOCAL to the segment (world beat - 32k):
-- these mirror the level data above; if `obs` changes, change these too
-- (and re-run the offline sim!)
jumps1 : List Int
jumps1 = [ 8, 12, 16, 20, 23, 24, 28 ]


jumps2 : List Int
jumps2 = [ 2, 4, 8, 11, 12, 14, 16, 20, 22, 24, 25, 26, 29 ]


jumps3 : List Int
jumps3 = [ 2, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 12, 14, 18, 20, 22, 24, 25, 26, 27, 30 ]


jumps4 : List Int
jumps4 = [ 2, 4, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 20, 22, 24, 25, 26, 28 ]


jumps5 : List Int
jumps5 = [ 2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10, 13, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24, 26, 27, 28, 30 ]


jumps6 : List Int
jumps6 = [ 2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 15, 18, 20, 22, 26, 28, 29, 30 ]


jumps7 : List Int
jumps7 = [ 2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24, 27, 29, 30, 31 ]


jumps8 : List Int
jumps8 = [ 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 17, 19, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 28, 30 ]


jumps9 : List Int
jumps9 = [ 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 13, 15, 17, 19, 20, 22, 24, 27, 29, 30, 31 ]


type alias LeadEv =
    { x : Int, hz : Int }


-- world positions where the game loop fires the melody: one note ON each
-- jump beat. The last two segments sing an octave up: the finale lift.
-- All positions are even, and the player moves 2 px/tick from even spawns,
-- so stepRun crosses each one exactly.
leadEvents : List LeadEv
leadEvents =
    let
        seg =
            \k jumps ->
                (List.foldl
                    (\b acc ->
                        let
                            bx = (k * 32 + b) * beatPx
                            oct = if k >= 4 then 2 else 1
                        in
                        { idx = acc.idx + 1
                        , evs =
                            List.concat
                                [ acc.evs
                                , [ { x = bx, hz = oct * leadPitch acc.idx } ]
                                ]
                        }
                    )
                    { idx = 0, evs = [] }
                    jumps).evs
    in
    List.concat
        [ seg 0 jumps1, seg 1 jumps2, seg 2 jumps3
        , seg 3 jumps4, seg 4 jumps5, seg 5 jumps6
        , seg 6 jumps7, seg 7 jumps8, seg 8 jumps9
        ]


-- one layer of the held note that ends the win jingle. Two of these, detuned
-- against each other, are the whole difference between an oscillator and a
-- thing being played.
winTail : Sound
winTail =
    Sound.vibrato 22 6 (Sound.volume 17 (Sound.duty 25 (Sound.tone Sound.Square (Sound.a 5) 420)))


cueSound : Cue -> Sound
cueSound cue =
    case cue of
        CueDeath ->
            -- The square shatters: a noise burst over a falling saw. An impact
            -- is the clearest case there is for a decay, and this one had none:
            -- 200 ms of hiss at a flat level is a sound effect, 200 ms of hiss
            -- that collapses in the first 130 is a thing breaking.
            --
            -- The saw already falls to 55 Hz, but a saw keeps its energy in the
            -- harmonics, so down there it was drawing the SHAPE of an impact
            -- without any of its weight. A sine on the same sweep, at 45% of
            -- the saw, is the weight. You hear this one a lot in this game;
            -- worth it being a hit rather than a noise.
            Sound.chord
                [ Sound.decay 130 0 (Sound.volume 34 (Sound.tone Sound.Noise 0 200))
                , Sound.decay 160 18 (Sound.volume 27 (Sound.sweep 55 (Sound.tone Sound.Sawtooth 320 220)))
                , Sound.decay 160 12 (Sound.volume 12 (Sound.sweep 55 (Sound.tone Sound.Sine 320 220)))
                ]

        CueCheck ->
            -- two plucks, not two blocks: a flag you pass, not a tone you play
            Sound.sequence
                [ Sound.decay 45 25 (Sound.volume 16 (Sound.duty 25 (Sound.tone Sound.Square (Sound.e 6) 60)))
                , Sound.decay 75 10 (Sound.volume 16 (Sound.duty 25 (Sound.tone Sound.Square (Sound.a 6) 90)))
                ]

        CueWin ->
            -- The three fanfare notes are struck and fall to about half, which
            -- is what makes them read as played rather than switched on. The
            -- last one is the payoff of the whole minute, so it gets what the
            -- lead gets: a unison PAIR seven cents apart, opened to either side
            -- of centre, falling slowly enough to breathe. Two beating copies
            -- add in quadrature rather than arithmetically, so each layer sits
            -- at 71% of the old single voice and the note stays the same size.
            Sound.sequence
                [ Sound.decay 70 55 (Sound.volume 24 (Sound.duty 25 (Sound.tone Sound.Square (Sound.c 5) 120)))
                , Sound.decay 70 55 (Sound.volume 24 (Sound.duty 25 (Sound.tone Sound.Square (Sound.e 5) 120)))
                , Sound.decay 70 55 (Sound.volume 24 (Sound.duty 25 (Sound.tone Sound.Square (Sound.g 5) 120)))
                , Sound.decay 260 62
                    (Sound.chord
                        [ Sound.pan (0 - leadPan) (Sound.detune (0 - 7) winTail)
                        , Sound.pan leadPan (Sound.detune 7 winTail)
                        ]
                    )
                ]

        CueLead hz ->
            -- the melody note, fired BY the world on the jump beat. LONG
            -- (440 ms ≈ the whole beat), and it rings out instead of stopping
            -- flat: without an explicit release the engine gives a voice 5 ms,
            -- which is only the floor below which a gain change clicks. That
            -- held the note at full level for 435 ms and then cut it, which is
            -- what made the high line sound dry.
            --
            -- 90 ms of release is about a fifth of the note, still short enough
            -- that a beat lands on silence rather than on the tail of the note
            -- before it. The decay is the other half of the same idea: the note
            -- falls to 70% over its first 240 ms and holds there, so a held
            -- note BREATHES instead of standing still. Both stages are shaped
            -- INSIDE the note (their ramps end at its own duration, they do not
            -- extend it), and both patch every voice of the chord, so all three
            -- layers stay exactly 440 ms and the chord keeps its alignment.
            --
            -- The unison used to be spelled in Hz: a second square at
            -- `hz + max 3 (hz // 90)`, which is roughly 16 cents SHARP of the
            -- melody and pulls the pitch centre up with it. Sound.detune says
            -- the same thing in the unit the ear actually uses, and says it
            -- symmetrically: seven cents down and seven cents up, beating
            -- against each other around a centre that is still the written
            -- note. Panning the pair to either side turns that beat into WIDTH,
            -- which is what makes two oscillators read as one instrument. The
            -- sub-octave triangle stays dead centre, because that is where the
            -- weight goes.
            --
            -- Each square sits at 12 rather than 13 and 9: two beating copies
            -- are incoherent and add in quadrature, so a matched pair measures
            -- about the same as the old lopsided one.
            Sound.release 90
                (Sound.decay 240 70
                    (Sound.chord
                        [ Sound.pan (0 - leadPan)
                            (Sound.detune (0 - 7)
                                (Sound.vibrato 14 5 (Sound.volume 12 (Sound.duty 50 (Sound.tone Sound.Square hz 440))))
                            )
                        , Sound.pan leadPan
                            (Sound.detune 7 (Sound.volume 12 (Sound.duty 50 (Sound.tone Sound.Square hz 440))))
                        , Sound.volume 7 (Sound.tone Sound.Triangle (hz // 2) 440)
                        ]
                    )
                )

        CueBeat b ->
            -- one beat of the groove, built for exactly this world position
            grooveBeat b
